Z||000000||fierohuddy||08-14-2001||12:38 PM||||I read some info a little while agofrom v8archie about the fact that you can't pump the clutch.  I have an 86 v6 and just did a clutch job and now that's the only way to get my clutch to work.  There is zero pressure after not shifting for say 2 minutes, then after about 5 or 6 pumps the damn thing works like a dream, until I take my foot off it for 2 minutes.  Looked for leaks and there doesn't appear to be any.<P>Any ideas,<P>Simon||||reg||9
Z||000001||ChadMan||08-14-2001||12:52 PM||||My guess is that you have a slight leak around the piston seal inside the master or slave cylinder.  The leak allows fluid to seep past the piston, but it never leaks out to the ground where you would see it.  When you pump it up, it builds enough pressure to move the clutch arm, but eventually seeps past the piston again.<P>It's probably the slave...I think if you had this type of leak with the clutch master cylinder, you'd see it seep out past the dust cover around the chrome-looking arm that attaches to the clutch pedal.<P>Good luck...||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierohuddy||08-14-2001||12:56 PM||||Can anyone help, pleeeeeeeeaase???!?!?!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||08-14-2001||01:03 PM||||Have you had to add any fluid?<P>Crawl under the dash and see if the master cylinder is leaking. If the seal starts going in them they leak inside the car. The fluid will just soak the carpet padding and you'll never notice it. ||||reg||1
